    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d281">

                <pb facs="0609" n="577" xml:id="pg577"/>

                <head>281. Memorandum From the President’s Special Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Bundy) to President
                        Kennedy<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d281-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-07-13">July 13,
                    1962</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>British Guiana</item>
                </list>
                <p>Here is a paper from Dean Rusk which comes
                    out hard for a policy of getting rid of Jagan.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d281-f2"/> It is a careful and thorough argument of one side. It has
                    more energy than most State Department papers.</p>
                <p>These documents seem to me to demonstrate that Jagan will
                    indeed go the way of Castro, if he is not prevented. He would be weaker than
                    Castro, because he is even more inefficient, but he would also probably be more
                    easily controlled from Moscow.</p>
                <p>But while the papers make a clear case against supporting Jagan, or even trying
                    to sustain peaceful coexistence with him, the case for the proposed tactics to
                    be used in opposing him is not so clear. In particular, I think it is unproven
                    that CIA knows how to manipulate an election in
                    British Guiana without a backfire.</p>
                <p>My immediate suggestion is that when you have read this, we should have a pretty
                    searching meeting on the details of the tactical plans, in which you can
                    cross-examine those who are really responsible for their development. I do not
                    think the Secretary of State should go to the British Ambassador with the
                    proposed talking paper until we are a little more sure of our own capabilities
                    and intentions.</p>
                <p>There is also a real question whether Dean
                        Rusk is the man to talk with the British on this. The last time
                    he told Home we could not put up with Jagan” the British
                    simply dug in their heels. Since British support for an
                        anti-Jagan policy would be the most powerful single
                    force for its success, I think you may want to go all out with David yourself on
                    this one.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">McG.
                            B.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
